  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showers and thunderstorms today with the risk of\nsome severe weather west of the Blue Ridge Mountains.\n\nWatching closely showers over Charles and southern Prince\nGeorges Counties where radar estimates indicate up to 2 inches\nof rain. Luckily, it is falling in places where FFG is higher\nthan compared to St. Mary's County. Once this cell moves out, we\nshould have a lull in precip until mid-afternoon. \n\nDuring the late morning and early afternoon hours there could be\na lull, but there remains a chance for pop-up showers and storms\npretty much anywhere during this period. \n\nBy evening, another round of showers and thunderstorms will\napproach the Appalachian Mountains and may make to areas east of\nthe Blue Ridge Mountains overnight into early Sunday. Severe\nthreat is low with these, as they will be running into stable\nsurface air. However, they could bring some heavy rainfall and\nthunder. Some fog may then develop in the valleys and VA\nPiedmont, but it will have to do so prior to the arrival of the\nfront. \n\nBy Sunday morning the cold front will be crossing the area with\ndry air advection ensuing in strengthening NW winds. However,\nthere could still be enough moisture and lift to spark a few\nshowers, but overall drying conditions are expected throughout\nthe day.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry conditions anticipated Sunday through early\nnext week. \n\nDewpoints drop sharply in the wake of the cold front Monday as dry \nair moves into the region from the Midwest. Surface high pressure \nwill begin to build over the Appalachians and track east over the \nnext couple of days, with conditions feeling briefly fall-like as \ntemperatures range slightly below average for August. Overnight\nlows range largely in the 50s throughout the region by Tuesday,\nand sub-60F dewpoints could persist until midweek.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Warmer and more humid conditions return mid-\nweek, with thunderstorm chances returning by Thursday.\n\nAs the week continues, a broad upper-level trough will begin to form \nover the Great Lakes and track towards the Mid-Atlantic by Thursday. \nAs the associated surface low approaches the region, moist air from \nthe south will flow into the area, bringing back an increase in \nhumidity & temperatures back to late-August normals. As this low \ntracks through, expect shower & thunderstorm chances to increase as \nthe associated cold front tracks offshore by the end of the week. \nMedium-range severe indices still maintain a low-end non-zero\nchance for stronger convection as this front moves through. \nTemperatures rise back into the upper 80s by Thursday.",
